\ exact-magnitude quantities — a quantity's magnitude may be an exact.
\ Arithmetic between two exact-magnitude quantities is exact end to end
\ (unit rescaling included); an exact magnitude against a float magnitude
\ errors; comparison crosses the two kinds exactly.

\ === attach, print, destructure ===

1/2 m . 1/3 $ . cr                            \ 1/2 m 1/3 $
1/2 m type-of . 1/2 m magnitude . cr          \ :quantity 1/2
10/3 km unit-of . cr                          \ 1 km
{ :price 1/3 } :price @ . cr                  \ 1/3

clear
\ === exact arithmetic with units ===

1/2 $ 50/1 ¢ + . 1/1 $ 25/1 ¢ - . cr          \ 1 $ 3/4 $
1/1 $ 3/1 / . 1/2 m 1/2 m * . cr              \ 1/3 $ 1/4 m^2
1/2 hour 1/1 s / . cr                         \ 1800 (dimensionless collapse is exact)
1/2 hour 2/1 m * . cr                         \ 3600 m.s (unnamed unit, scale folded exactly)
1/2 $ negate . -7/2 m abs . cr                \ -1/2 $ 7/2 m
3/2 $ 2 ^ . 2/1 m -2 ^ . 1/2 $ 3 ^ . cr       \ 9/4 $^2 1/4 m^-2 1/8 $^3
3/2 $ 2/1 ^ . cr                              \ 9/4 $^2 (an integer exact exponent works too)
1/2 $ "{0} each" format . cr                  \ 1/2 $ each (format renders quantities)
10 km "{0}" format . 1/2 hour 2/1 m * "{0}" format . cr  \ 10 km 3600 m.s
1/10 $ 2/10 $ + 3/10 $ = . cr                 \ 1 (0.1 $ 0.2 $ + 0.3 $ = is 0)

clear
\ === comparison crosses exact and float magnitudes ===

1/2 $ 0.5 $ = . 100/1 ¢ 1/1 $ = . cr          \ 1 1
0.4 $ 1/2 $ < . 1/2 $ 0.4 $ < . cr            \ 1 0
1/2 $ 40/1 ¢ > . cr                           \ 1
1/2 $ 1/3 $ max2 . 1/2 $ 0.6 $ min2 . cr      \ 1/2 $ 1/2 $
[ 1/2 $ 2/1 $ 0.75 $ ] sort . cr              \ [ 1/2 $ 0.75 $ 2 $ ]

clear
\ === errors (each reason parenthesized) ===

1 m 1/2 m +                                   \ (exact and float magnitudes do not mix)
1/2 m 3 *                                     \ (a bare float against an exact magnitude)
1/2 $ 0/1 $ /                                 \ (division by zero)
1/2 $ 0.5 ^                                   \ (the exponent must be an integer)
1/2 $ 1/2 ^                                   \ (an exact exponent must be an integer too)
10 m 2/1 ^                                    \ (an exact exponent on a float magnitude mixes)
1/4 $ sqrt                                    \ (sqrt rejects an exact magnitude)
clear
